{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"apps/clax_app/lib/daemon/supervisor.dart","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:09:59.609721Z","id":"4b25efefdb8879b92e60af452d87195b3e784ffeadb601994b4c655c5aeec0ba","body":{"detail":"_stopStale sends SIGTERM (then SIGKILL) to daemon.json's pid when PidKiller.alive says it exists. The supervisor deletes daemon.json when its own child exits (_forgetDaemonJson), so the file outlives its daemon only when the app itself was killed while the daemon kept running, or the daemon was killed while the app was not running. In that window the PID can be reused: on macOS by any process of the same user, which would be signalled. On Android, other apps' processes are hidden from /proc and cannot be signalled, so only this app's own processes are exposed.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":380},"end":{"line":388},"content_hash":"be7c02039e5707018cc658260314b796d0675412d387bb79b20ac880106a6017"},"suggested_fix":"Before signalling, confirm the process is the daemon: GET /healthz on daemon.json's port and compare its pid, or compare the process's executable (/proc/<pid>/exe on Android, `ps -o comm= -p <pid>` on macOS) with the located clax; signal only a match or an unanswering process whose executable is clax.","summary":"The stale-daemon stop signals whatever process holds the PID daemon.json names, without checking it is clax","tags":["risk","daemon","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"apps/clax_app/lib/daemon/process.dart","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:09:59.609799Z","id":"ac797ec904426eabec665d503877fd905235f8de66f0dfdd2808f687b681b615","body":{"detail":"`clax serve --foreground` logs through tracing_subscriber::fmt() (crates/clax-cli/src/commands/serve.rs), which writes to stdout; IoProcessStarter drains stdout and drops it. The tail that Failed carries (supervisor.dart _keepTail) therefore has what reaches stderr: panics and the `Error: …` a failing main prints, but not warnings such as a port fallback or a store problem logged before exit.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":50},"end":{"line":51},"content_hash":"16f00d39642324b163ad4aca873c5560bbf4760cd5701420e8ff23bdff58086f"},"suggested_fix":"Either keep a tail of stdout too (a second ring, or both streams merged into the one the Failed state shows), or have serve log to stderr when started by the app.","summary":"The daemon's log lines go to stdout, which is discarded, so the Failed screen's tail holds only stderr","tags":["daemon","diagnostics","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"apps/clax_app/lib/daemon/supervisor.dart","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:09:59.609845Z","id":"0a396b836e2eabf84523484ac0b23fae390079d9ba8db53cff0963dc1b20e7ed","body":{"detail":"Each such start takes the 15 s health limit plus its backoff (supervisor.dart healthLimit, backoff), so failed starts land at least 15 s apart and at most four fall within the 60 s failWindow; the five-in-sixty rule (spec §3.1 'Death') never trips. The person sees Starting/Restarting indefinitely instead of the error screen with Restart and the stderr tail. A daemon that exits at once does reach Failed (test 'a waiter of ensureUp gets the failure').","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":250},"end":{"line":265},"content_hash":"e6bd7db611f075e9ef9b14dc7ca65b4acc50cc05aa1001a8b2908cd69d1df139"},"suggested_fix":"Count consecutive failed starts separately (for example, Failed after three health timeouts in a row), or measure the failure window from the start of each attempt rather than its end.","summary":"A daemon that starts but never answers /healthz restarts forever and never reaches Failed","tags":["daemon","spec-gap","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"apps/clax_app/lib/daemon/supervisor.dart","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-12T06:09:59.609880Z","id":"9fbb9e0347073d4aa5b9dac180a1b65cf8102889079488a643c3dad3f44ba323","body":{"detail":"The daemon writes daemon.json only after Store::open_reporting returns (crates/clax-server/src/daemon.rs serve), and while it opens it reports progress in starting.json; the CLI's own spawn moves its deadline on while that progress advances (crates/clax-cli/src/client.rs Client::spawn, StartWatch). The app's supervisor uses a fixed 15 s (spec §3.1 'Up'), so a migration or audit backfill longer than that is SIGKILLed mid-open and retried from the start. A phone's fresh home opens well within it; a long-lived home after an upgrade may not.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":129},"end":{"line":129},"content_hash":"7ec891da1b7af5eb42fb9145ba1e3da4a35eed55d565d7dab7e835e3302c12ad"},"suggested_fix":"Extend the deadline while <home>/starting.json names the started PID and its heartbeat_at advances, as Client::spawn does.","summary":"The fixed 15 s health limit ignores starting.json, so a slow first open of a large home counts as a failed start and is killed","tags":["daemon","spec-gap","session:claude-code:e697d918-f48a-41f9-aac4-4be74128e8ae"]}}
